# AICA frontend configuration — copy to `.env` and fill in.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Every key here is read in exactly one place, src/lib/env.ts, and nowhere
# else in the app. Vite only exposes names starting with VITE_, and it INLINES
# them into the JavaScript bundle at build time: anything set here is readable
# by anyone who opens the deployed site. Never put a real secret in this file.
#
# Load order (later wins): .env → .env.[mode] → .env.local → .env.[mode].local
# On Vercel, `.env` is NOT deployed — set the same keys in
# Project → Settings → Environment Variables, then redeploy.

# ─── Backend connection ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# REQUIRED for a live test call. The agent backend's /ws/audio endpoint.
# Local dev:  ws://localhost:8000/ws/audio
# Production: wss://<your-backend-host>/ws/audio   (wss, not ws — a browser on
#             an https:// page refuses to open a plaintext ws:// socket)
# Unset ⇒ the rest of the console still works; Simulation & Testing → Run
# simulation shows "VITE_BACKEND_WS_URL is not set" instead of connecting.
VITE_BACKEND_WS_URL=ws://localhost:8000/ws/audio

# Optional. Origin of the backend's REST API — GET /api/health (which parts of
# the pipeline actually loaded) and GET /api/calls (the replayable call log).
# Derived from VITE_BACKEND_WS_URL by default, since the backend serves both
# from one process: ws://localhost:8000/ws/audio -> http://localhost:8000.
# Set this only if the two genuinely differ.
#
# NOTE: these are the only cross-origin requests the app makes. A browser
# applies CORS to fetch() but NOT to WebSockets, so test calls can connect and
# stream perfectly while the health pills stay grey and the call log stays
# empty. To fix it, either set CORS_ORIGINS on the backend to this frontend's
# origin, or serve both behind one proxy and point VITE_BACKEND_WS_URL at a
# relative path (/ws/audio), which makes everything same-origin.
# VITE_BACKEND_HTTP_URL=http://localhost:8000

# Optional. Set only when the backend has AUDIO_WS_AUTH_TOKEN configured
# (backend/settings.py → SecuritySettings). Sent as ?token= on the handshake,
# since a browser WebSocket cannot set request headers. Leave unset for local
# dev, where the backend ships with auth off. Treat this as a low-value gate,
# not a secret — it ships in the bundle. Real protection belongs at the
# backend's network edge (origin allow-list, IP rules).
# VITE_BACKEND_WS_TOKEN=

# ─── Call contract — must match the backend ──────────────────────────────────

# Which language the test-call panel's picker starts on. Language is fixed for
# the life of a connection (it is sent in `call_started` and there is no
# mid-call switch), and must be one the backend's ASR has actually loaded, or
# the call opens and transcribes nothing. Default: ta
# VITE_CALL_LANGUAGE=ta

# MIC CAPTURE rate, in Hz. Must equal the backend's ASR input rate exactly:
# the handshake rejects anything else, and a mismatch that slips through is
# silent — the audio plays back at the wrong speed and transcribes as noise.
# Not the playback rate: the agent's voice comes back at whatever rate
# `agent_speaking_start` announces (24000 with the default TTS engine), read
# from the wire rather than configured. Default: 16000
# VITE_AUDIO_SAMPLE_RATE=16000

# ─── Support contact (Help & Contact page) ───────────────────────────────────

# The desk that actually answers for this deployment. Defaults are BitnTech's.
# VITE_SUPPORT_EMAIL=support@bitntech.com
# VITE_SUPPORT_PHONE=+1 (415) 555-0148
